        moat: "A low cost position built on integration: 79% of its power comes from its own plants at ₹2.65 a unit against ₹5–7 from the grid, and it can sell intermediates or use them itself. The weakness is that steel is priced by the market: its carbon-steel realisation rose only 2% in Q1 FY27.",
        salesDrivers: ["**Steel prices and construction demand**: carbon steel is the largest line, so bar and billet prices drive profit. Secondary rebar prices fell in July 2026 and carbon-steel long volumes grew only 2.8% in August.", "**Higher-value products**: stainless steel, alloys and foil earn far more a tonne than intermediates. In Q1 FY27 realisations rose 27% in stainless and 32% in foil, against 2% in carbon steel.", "**New plants**: FY27 brings a foil plant, 0.5 million tonnes of iron making, power plants and a railway-wagon plant; the big ones (a ₹5,400cr hot strip mill with ₹169cr spent, the ₹2,940cr stainless flat chain and a ₹900cr bar mill) come mostly in FY28–FY29.", "**Guidance**: on the July 2026 call management guided FY27 revenue, volume and operating-profit growth of “more than 20–25%” (in May it said “close to 30%”); Vision 2031 targets revenue of about ₹42,500cr and operating profit of about ₹6,200cr.", "**Exports**: about 21% of FY26 revenue, led by aluminium foil."],
